WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.180
On the fourth chapter of the live-action Star
Wars series The Mandalorian, entitled "Sanctuary,"

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.880
the series gave fans another reason to love
the popular series when it introduced a character

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.700
named Omera.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.430
Here's why Julia Jones, the actress who plays
her, looks so familiar.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.520
Though it's one of the actress' lesser-known
projects, the Larry Bishop-directed, Western-tinged

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.840
biker opus Hell Ride is a bold entry in Julia
Jones' filmography.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.570
Released in 2008 and produced by none other
than Quentin Tarantino, Hell Ride follows

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.050
Bishop and Michael Madsen as heads of the
Victors biker gang, and finds them entrenched

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.440
in a bloody showdown with David Carradine's
own Six-Six-Six's gang.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.890
Envisioned as the greatest biker movie ever
made by Tarantino and Bishop, Hell Ride doesn't

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:51.370
quite live up to those lofty ambitions, but
it's every bit as violent, macho, and subversively

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.720
silly as its setup would have you believe.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.660
Hell Ride's B-movie absurdities make for a
little slice of grindhouse heaven for cinema

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.660
lovers.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.690
If you're among them, you're certain to recognize
Jones for her turn as Cherokee Kisum, the

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.920
doomed paramour of Bishop's Pistolero.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.470
While Jones doesn't play a major role in the
film her character gets offed early in Hell

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:17.610
Ride's gritty action she makes the most of
the moment in what was just her third feature

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.520
film, ensuring that Cherokee Kisum's spirit
casts a long shadow of the film's narrative.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.810
Hell Ride may not have boosted Julia Jones'
profile to household name status during the

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.170
early days of her career, but it did get her
enough notice to land a role in a tentpole

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.630
franchise featuring a certain sparkly-skinned
vampire.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:40.500
2010's The Twilight Saga: Eclipse qualified
as Jones' first big mainstream break, with

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.380
fans around the world taking a liking to her
character Leah Clearwater.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.100
"If you're here to torture Jacob some more,
feel free to leave."

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.380
"Fun, isn't she?"

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.700
As a refresher, Eclipse was the first Twilight
film to really spend some time with the vampire-hating,

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.409
shape-shifting wolf-folk that Bella's bestie-slash-would-be
beau Jacob ran around with through much of

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:04.049
the series.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:07.500
Among those werewolves are Leah and Seth Clearwater.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.120
Jones' Leah is extremely powerful, and was
the only woman among the wolf-people to ever

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.870
take the form of the animal.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:19.110
As such, she was immediately named the second-in-command
to Jacob's Alpha within the pack.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.160
Though there were a lot of overarching narratives
and tons of intricate lore going on in the

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.459
film, all you really need to know about Eclipse
is that Julia Jones was in it and that she

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.701
was really good in what amounted to a relatively
thankless role that she continued to play

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:37.990
over the course of the final two, increasingly
ridiculous Twilight flicks, Breaking Dawn

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.680
Parts 1 and 2.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.019
Luckily, the rather complicated reputation
of the Twilight movies didn't prevent Jones

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:47.370
from moving on to better if not bigger things.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:53.170
In fact, one of Jones' most memorable supporting
turns came in the decidedly mid-budget 2017

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.620
thriller Wind River.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:59.640
Written and directed by Taylor Sheridan, Wind
River unfolds in the icy vistas of Montana,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.630
and sees a troubled tracker named Cory Lambert
played by Jeremy Renner attempting to navigate

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:09.060
local and tribal laws while untangling a web
of mysteries surrounding the death of his

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.319
friend's daughter on a local reservation.

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:15.860
We come to find out that the case is of particular
interest to Cory because his own daughter

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.890
had been killed in a troubling case years
earlier.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:23.050
In hopes of providing some answers for his
old friend, Cory tackles the case head-on

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.150
and stirs up some still-raging emotions within
himself on the way.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.790
Those emotions are never closer to the surface
as they are when Cory visits Wilma, the mother

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.790
of his deceased child and his ex-wife, who
is memorably played with raw-nerve energy

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.400
by you guessed it Julia Jones.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.069
"You've got blood on your shirt."

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:44.120
"Yeah, I just came from work."

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.709
"Who was the victim today?"

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:52.379
Though her turn as Wilma in Wind River was
short, Jones still brought some serious fire

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:53.480
to the mix.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.091
She rendered a fully-formed character from
the abstract fragments of Wilma, and the character

00:03:58.091 --> 00:04:01.720
is every bit as wounded and haunted by the
past as Cory.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:07.220
As far as supporting turns go, Jones' work
in Wind River isn't quite of the legit scene-stealing

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.830
nature, but it's about as close as one can
get.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.570
The Mandalorian is hardly Julia Jones' first
foray into television.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.720
She got her first taste of the small-screen
big time with a four-episode arc as Dr. Kaya

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.740
Montoya on the final season of E.R. back in
2008.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:29.130
Jones also delivered solid work as Gabriella
Langton on A&amp;E's Western series Longmire,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:33.710
and can currently be seen as Stephanie Littlecrow
on the Billy Bob Thornton-starring legal drama

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:34.889
Goliath.

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:40.009
Perhaps Jones' most memorable TV appearance
was as Kohana on the second season of HBO's

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:43.680
daring, perplexing sci-fi Western Westworld.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.400
As complex and frustrating as Westworld season
2 often was, when the series took a moment

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.330
to slow down, it offered wildly inventive,
vividly rendered, jaw-droppingly beautiful

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:58.710
glimpses into some of the more intriguing
corners of Westworld at large.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.780
One such corner belonged to the doomed park's
indigenous people, the Ghost Nation.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.180
And one of those astounding moments came on
the eighth episode of Westworld season 2.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.620
Entitled "Kiksuya," the episode proved a bold
narrative diversion from the relative chaos

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.180
of the season, and followed Zahn McClarnon's
Akecheta as he narrates the Ghost Nation's

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.960
rise to consciousness.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.890
He does so through the telling of his own
dramatic story — a harrowing one of a love

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.710
lost and then tragically found.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:32.020
That story finds Akecheta falling passionately
in love with fellow Ghost Nationer Kohana,

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.960
only to have her ripped away from him after
both characters are reprogrammed by the park's

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:36.990
storytellers.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:41.850
As the story progresses with Akecheta and
Kohana's lives crossing in increasingly devastating

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:48.169
fashion, it does so with almost no dialogue
from Kohana a fact that makes Jones' silently

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:53.729
emotive work in the role all the more remarkable,
and that adds unimaginable weight to her reading

00:05:53.729 --> 00:05:55.420
of the episode's signature line:

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:59.030
"Mi cante ki yu ha ya ye."

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.130
"Take my heart when you go."

